"
module queue_train #(
    parameter DATA_WIDTH,
    parameter ADDR_WIDTH,
    parameter NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES,

    parameter FRONT_ASYNC,
    parameter END_ASYNC,

    parameter PUSH_SIMPLE,
    parameter PUSH_BURST_SIZE,
    parameter POP_BURSTMARK,

    parameter CONFLICT_PROOF,
    parameter REGISTERED_IN,
    parameter REGISTERED_IN_BRAM,
    parameter REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM,
    parameter SYNC_STAGES
)
"
- DATA_WIDTH and ADDR_WIDTH apply uniformly to every queue_carriage in the train
- NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES controls the number of generated queue_carriage instances and must
  be at least 1
- FRONT_ASYNC and END_ASYNC select the location of the transition from the write clock
  domain to the read clock domain
- for NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES greater than 1, at most one async selector may be enabled because
  only two clock domains exist and the chain can contain only one transition between them
- for NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES equal to 1, either async selector makes the sole carriage
  asynchronous because that carriage is both the front and the end
- PUSH_SIMPLE applies to every carriage push coupler
- PUSH_BURST_SIZE is used only by the front carriage; internal carriage links use 1
- POP_BURSTMARK is used only by the end carriage; internal carriage links use 1
- the queue implementation parameters are forwarded uniformly to every carriage
- parameter legality is inherited from queue_carriage and its contained modules
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
    // Write side
    input wr_clk_i,
    input wr_rst_i,

    input  [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] wr_data_i,
    input                       wr_valid_i,
    output                      wr_ready_o,
    output                      wr_burstready_o,

    // Read side
    input rd_clk_i,
    input rd_rst_i,

    output [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] rd_data_o,
    output                      rd_valid_o,
    output                      rd_burstvalid_o,
    input                       rd_ready_i
"
- the external interface contains only the write and read clock domains
- no separate shared/internal clock or reset is required
- wr_data_i, wr_valid_i, wr_ready_o and wr_burstready_o form the upstream interface of the
  front carriage
- rd_data_o, rd_valid_o, rd_burstvalid_o and rd_ready_i form the downstream interface of
  the end carriage
- both external datapaths are exactly DATA_WIDTH bits wide
- when an async selector is enabled, wr_clk_i/wr_rst_i and rd_clk_i/rd_rst_i are assumed
  to represent distinct clock domains and queue_async CDC constraints apply
- when both async selectors are disabled, the user must drive both clock ports with the
  same clock and both reset ports with the same reset
- every active domain must receive several reset cycles followed by several idle cycles
  before traffic begins
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
    logic [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES : 0][DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] carriage_data;
    logic [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES : 0]                    carriage_valid;
    logic [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES : 0]                    carriage_ready;

    logic [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1 : 0] carriage_burstready;
    logic [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1 : 0] carriage_burstvalid;
"
- carriage_data, carriage_valid and carriage_ready represent interfaces at carriage
  boundaries rather than individual carriages
- N carriages have N + 1 boundaries: external input boundary 0, N - 1 internal boundaries,
  and external output boundary N
- [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES : 0] therefore intentionally contains NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES + 1
  entries and is not an off-by-one packed-range error
- each carriage has one burstready and one burstvalid output, so those arrays correctly
  contain NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES entries using [NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1 : 0]
- every carriage_data entry is DATA_WIDTH bits wide
[Marc103 + Codex 5.6 - 08/13/26]

"
    assign carriage_data[0]  = wr_data_i;
    assign carriage_valid[0] = wr_valid_i;
    assign wr_ready_o         = carriage_ready[0];
    assign wr_burstready_o    = carriage_burstready[0];

    assign rd_data_o                           = carriage_data[NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES];
    assign rd_valid_o                          = carriage_valid[NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES];
    assign rd_burstvalid_o                     = carriage_burstvalid[NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1];
    assign carriage_ready[NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES] = rd_ready_i;
"
- the external write data and valid signals drive boundary 0
- ready propagates backward from carriage 0 to the external write interface
- only carriage 0's burstready is externally meaningful because it is calculated using
  PUSH_BURST_SIZE
- the final boundary at index NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES drives external read data and valid
- only the final carriage's burstvalid is externally meaningful because it is calculated
  using POP_BURSTMARK
- external rd_ready_i enters at boundary NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES and propagates backward
  through each ready/valid connection
- these assignments also remain valid for NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES equal to 1
[Marc103 + Codex 5.6 - 08/13/26]

"
    generate
        for(genvar carriage = 0; carriage < NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES; carriage++) begin : carriage_g
            localparam CARRIAGE_ASYNC = (
                ((carriage == 0) && (FRONT_ASYNC == 1)) ||
                ((carriage == (NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1)) && (END_ASYNC == 1))
            ) ? 1 : 0;
"
- the generate loop creates exactly NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES instances at indices 0 through
  NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1
- CARRIAGE_ASYNC is 1 for the first carriage when FRONT_ASYNC is enabled
- CARRIAGE_ASYNC is 1 for the last carriage when END_ASYNC is enabled
- for a one-carriage train, the first and last indices are the same, so either selector
  correctly makes the sole carriage asynchronous
- the ternary converts the constant logical expression to an explicit integer 0 or 1
- for trains longer than one carriage, enabling both selectors is outside the documented
  parameter contract
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
            localparam CARRIAGE_PUSH_BURST_SIZE =
                (carriage == 0) ? PUSH_BURST_SIZE : 1;

            localparam CARRIAGE_POP_BURSTMARK =
                (carriage == (NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1)) ? POP_BURSTMARK : 1;
"
- only the front carriage receives PUSH_BURST_SIZE because wr_burstready_o describes
  burst admission into the complete train
- every later push coupler receives a burst size of 1 and participates only in ordinary
  ready/valid transfer between adjacent carriages
- only the end carriage receives POP_BURSTMARK because rd_burstvalid_o describes the
  amount of output data available at the complete train's endpoint
- every earlier pop coupler receives a burst mark of 1 and therefore exposes ordinary
  item availability to its downstream carriage
- no internal burstready or burstvalid signal is used to qualify the ready/valid chain
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
            queue_carriage #(
                .DATA_WIDTH(DATA_WIDTH),
                .ADDR_WIDTH(ADDR_WIDTH),
                .ASYNC(CARRIAGE_ASYNC),
                .PUSH_SIMPLE(PUSH_SIMPLE),
                .PUSH_BURST_SIZE(CARRIAGE_PUSH_BURST_SIZE),
                .POP_BURSTMARK(CARRIAGE_POP_BURSTMARK),
                .CONFLICT_PROOF(CONFLICT_PROOF),
                .REGISTERED_IN(REGISTERED_IN),
                .REGISTERED_IN_BRAM(REGISTERED_IN_BRAM),
                .REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM(REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM),
                .SYNC_STAGES(SYNC_STAGES)
            ) carriage_inst (
"
- every queue_carriage receives the common data width, address width, push implementation,
  queue registration configuration and synchronization depth
- CARRIAGE_ASYNC selects queue or queue_async independently at the chosen train endpoint
- CARRIAGE_PUSH_BURST_SIZE and CARRIAGE_POP_BURSTMARK implement endpoint-only burst
  semantics without changing the internal ready/valid protocol
- if the front carriage is asynchronous, PUSH_SIMPLE is 1 and PUSH_BURST_SIZE is greater
  than 1, wr_burstready_o remains 0 because queue_async supplies only one-entry lookahead
- that unsupported burst-admission combination does not disable wr_ready_o or ordinary
  single-item transfers
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
                .wr_clk_i(
                    ((carriage == 0) || (FRONT_ASYNC == 0))
                        ? wr_clk_i
                        : rd_clk_i
                ),
                .wr_rst_i(
                    ((carriage == 0) || (FRONT_ASYNC == 0))
                        ? wr_rst_i
                        : rd_rst_i
                ),
"
- carriage 0 always accepts the external write interface in the write domain
- when FRONT_ASYNC is 0, all carriage write sides remain in the write domain
- when FRONT_ASYNC is 1, carriage 0 crosses into the read domain and every following
  carriage write side uses rd_clk_i/rd_rst_i
- the reset selection exactly follows the selected write-side clock domain
- all conditions are elaboration-time constants, so these selections synthesize as direct
  connections rather than runtime clock multiplexers
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
                .wr_data_i(carriage_data[carriage]),
                .wr_valid_i(carriage_valid[carriage]),
                .wr_ready_o(carriage_ready[carriage]),
                .wr_burstready_o(carriage_burstready[carriage]),
"
- carriage index i consumes data and valid from boundary i
- its ready output drives the same boundary and therefore propagates back toward the
  external producer
- each carriage burstready is retained so index 0 can drive wr_burstready_o
- internal burstready values are intentionally not used for carriage-to-carriage transfer
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
                .rd_clk_i(
                    ((carriage == (NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1)) || (FRONT_ASYNC == 1))
                        ? rd_clk_i
                        : wr_clk_i
                ),
                .rd_rst_i(
                    ((carriage == (NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES - 1)) || (FRONT_ASYNC == 1))
                        ? rd_rst_i
                        : wr_rst_i
                ),
"
- the final carriage always presents the external output interface in the read domain
- with FRONT_ASYNC enabled, every carriage read side uses the read domain; the crossing is
  therefore confined to carriage 0
- with FRONT_ASYNC disabled, every non-final carriage read side uses the write domain
- when END_ASYNC is enabled under that configuration, the crossing is confined to the
  final carriage
- when neither async option is enabled, the final synchronous carriage receives wr_clk_i
  on its write side and rd_clk_i on its read side; the interface contract requires those
  clocks and resets to be driven identically
- all selections are elaboration-time constants and do not infer runtime clock muxes
[Marc103 - 08/13/26]

"
                .rd_data_o(carriage_data[carriage + 1]),
                .rd_valid_o(carriage_valid[carriage + 1]),
                .rd_burstvalid_o(carriage_burstvalid[carriage]),
                .rd_ready_i(carriage_ready[carriage + 1])
            );
        end
    endgenerate
"
- carriage index i produces data and valid directly at boundary i + 1
- the next carriage consumes that same boundary without additional registers or
  combinational qualification
- ready at boundary i + 1 flows into carriage i and provides downstream backpressure
- the final generated carriage drives boundary NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES, which is connected to
  the external read interface
- every carriage burstvalid is retained so the final index can drive rd_burstvalid_o
- queue_pop_coupler therefore interfaces directly with the following
  queue_push_coupler
[Marc103 + Codex 5.6 - 08/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// AI verification summary
- exactly NUMBER_OF_CARRIAGES queue_carriage instances are generated
- N carriages are connected by N + 1 correctly sized ready/valid/data boundaries
- all data remains DATA_WIDTH bits wide and transaction order is preserved
- the train contains one write-to-read clock-domain transition when one async endpoint is
  enabled, and no asynchronous carriage when both selectors are disabled
- front and end burst behavior is isolated to the corresponding external endpoint
- adjacent queue_carriage instances are connected directly without additional logic
- synchronous configurations require the user to drive matching clocks and resets
- asynchronous configurations assume distinct domains and inherit queue_async CDC rules
- reset and post-reset idle requirements are part of the integration contract
- lint elaboration passed for one-carriage, synchronous, front-async and end-async cases
[Marc103 + Codex 5.6 - 08/13/26]
